{"id": "9feec431-eeef-4848-89b8-1083f55a210a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:28:44.432935", "source": "user", "parent_id": "2e354796-8964-46d5-8314-ff8f617e6b1e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Initial inspection request. Please perform the following and report back WITHOUT proposing or producing the full experiment design yet. (1) Repository / input inspection: list and briefly describe what is present under /workspace that is relevant to the question (TorchOpt source, TorchViz source or stubs, any existing benchmark or graph-dump scripts, any models/optimizers already wired up, and any documentation describing TorchOpt's fused-optimizer behavior and TorchViz's tracing behavior). Note exact paths and a short note per item on what it contains and whether it is usable as-is. Do not modify any files. (2) Proposed approach (sketch only): how you would empirically compare the gradient graph produced by TorchOpt's fused-Adam path against the graph produced by TorchViz when tracing the unfused equivalent; which metrics you would compute (node count, edge count, op counts by category, graph depth, presence of duplicated subgraphs), which visualization/counting tooling you would use, what model(s) and input shape(s) you would trace, and how you would keep the two comparisons controlled (same model, same forward/backward semantics, only the optimizer/tracing path differs). (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties: call out anything you are assuming because it is not pinned down in the supplied context — for example, what 'less complex and more interpretable' will be operationalized as, what constitutes a fair control between the two tools, whether TorchViz is being used as a passive tracer of standard PyTorch autograd or as the source of the unfused graph, whether fused-Adam in TorchOpt is expected to reduce node count by collapsing update steps into the backward graph or only the parameter update step, and any dependency/runtime risks given the bubblewrap, no-network, system-Python environment. Do not run the full experiment; spend compute only on read-only inspection (ls, find, grep, head, wc) needed to answer (1). Return your inspection findings, approach sketch, and assumptions list.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"Does fusing operations within the Adam optimizer (as done in TorchOpt) result in a less complex and more interpretable gradient graph compared to the output from TorchViz?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
